  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $\\frac{9}{4}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{2\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{3}{2}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
